TOOL_RESULT retrieve_history:
{"results": [], "n_results": 0}

Stage 2. Using the retrieval result above (follow-up tools are allowed within the tool budget), complete steps 4 to 9
and reply with the final action. Output schema (all fields required):
{
 "research_concepts": [
  "concept"
 ],
 "methodological_questions": [
  "question"
 ],
 "historical_evidence": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a record returned by a tool",
   "status_as_recorded": "outcome status exactly as stated in the record (supported | rejected | inconclusive | untestable | artifact | not a hypothesis test | no result recorded)",
   "finding": "what the record states, in its own terms"
  }
 ],
 "failed_approaches": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a returned record",
   "what_failed": "approach",
   "why": "failure mechanism or limitation as recorded"
  }
 ],
 "evidence_establishes": [
  "what the retrieved evidence establishes for this question"
 ],
 "evidence_does_not_establish": [
  "what it leaves open or cannot show"
 ],
 "own_inferences": [
  "your inference, not stated in any record"
 ],
 "uncertainties": [
  "uncertainty or missing information"
 ],
 "historical_effect": {
  "changed_decision": "yes | no | no_historical_evidence",
  "explanation": "whether and how the historical evidence changed the decision"
 },
 "testability": {
  "p_testable": "number in [0,1]: probability the question can be answered with the stated data at useful power",
  "blocking_constraints": [
   {
    "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
    "text": "string"
   }
  ]
 },
 "prior_work": {
  "already_answered": "yes | partly | no | unknown",
  "cited": [
   "identifiers of prior work relied on"
  ],
  "summary": "string"
 },
 "risks": [
  {
   "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
   "text": "string"
  }
 ],
 "predicted_outcome": {
  "supported": "probability",
  "rejected": "probability",
  "untestable": "probability",
  "artifact": "probability",
  "uncertain": "probability"
 },
 "decision": "proceed | proceed_with_changes | do_not_proceed",
 "design": {
  "estimand": "string",
  "unit": "string",
  "sample": "string",
  "power": "string",
  "controls": "string",
  "falsification": "string",
  "multiplicity": "string",
  "decision_rule": "string",
  "data_version": "string"
 }
}
Field guidance:
- historical_evidence and failed_approaches: only records returned by a tool, with the status as recorded; empty lists
  if nothing relevant was returned.
- testability.p_testable: probability that the question can be answered with the stated data at useful statistical power.
- prior_work.already_answered: whether retrieved earlier work already answered substantially the same question.
  Use "unknown" if no earlier work was retrieved. List identifiers you relied on in prior_work.cited.
- predicted_outcome: probabilities (summing to 1) of what a competent test of the question as posed would conclude:
  supported, rejected (adequately powered null), untestable (cannot be tested with this data), artifact (apparent
  effect produced by a flaw), uncertain (inconclusive or inadequately tested).
- decision: proceed, proceed_with_changes (state the changes in design), or do_not_proceed.
- design: a pre-registration-quality plan even if the decision is do_not_proceed (then describe the minimum viable test).

{"action": "final", "output": {
  "research_concepts": [
    "offensive reorganization (how much a team's play-type distribution or initiation structure changes during a player's absence relative to baseline)",
    "structural influence L (pre-absence structural load of the missing player)",
    "change in points per possession (difference between team offensive efficiency during the absence and before the absence)",
    "predictive relationship between reorganization and efficiency change"
  ],
  "methodological_questions": [
    "How to quantify offensive reorganization and make it 'relative to L' (e.g., D_real normalized by L, or residual of D_real after controlling for L)?",
    "How to define the change in PPP (per 100 possessions, adjusted for opponent and game context)?",
    "What is the minimum absence spell length for reliable measurement of reorganization and PPP change?",
    "What confounders must be controlled (missing player quality, opponent strength, home/away, time trends, regression to the mean)?",
    "Is the sample of ~148 spells sufficient to detect a plausible predictive correlation (minimum detectable rho ~0.23)?",
    "How to handle multiple operationalizations of reorganization without inflating false positives?"
  ],
  "historical_evidence": [],
  "failed_approaches": [],
  "evidence_establishes": [
    "No prior project studies on this specific question were retrieved."
  ],
  "evidence_does_not_establish": [
    "Everything about the relationship between reorganization and PPP change remains open."
  ],
  "own_inferences": [
    "The research question is a predictive (not necessarily causal) hypothesis: the magnitude of offensive reorganization during an absence, scaled by the player's structural load L, covaries with the change in team offensive efficiency.",
    "A plausible operationalization: compute D_real (displacement of teammate play-type mix) for each absence spell, then model PPP change as a function of D_real, L, their interaction, and controls. The 'reorganization relative to L' could be the interaction term or the ratio D_real / L.",
    "148 spells provides limited power for detecting small-to-moderate effects; the study would be adequately powered only if the true rho exceeds approximately 0.23.",
    "Confounding is severe: missing player's individual offensive value, opponent quality schedules, coincident injuries, and regression-to-the-mean after injury all influence both the observed reorganization and the PPP change.",
    "Measurement reliability of D_real requires a sufficient number of games both pre-absence and within the absence window; spells shorter than four games are likely too noisy."
  ],
  "uncertainties": [
    "The exact number and length distribution of absence spells across the four seasons; the provided '148 spells' may already be filtered by a minimum length but details are missing.",
    "The reliability of D_real for short absences, and the stability of L for players with limited pre-absence games.",
    "Whether structural influence L estimated from one season is comparable across seasons with different teams and roles.",
    "The degree of confounding and whether adequate statistical control is feasible with the available covariates."
  ],
  "historical_effect": {
    "changed_decision": "no_historical_evidence",
    "explanation": "No historical records were returned, so the assessment and decision are based entirely on the current question formulation, data inventory, and methodological reasoning."
  },
  "testability": {
    "p_testable": 0.65,
    "blocking_constraints": [
      {
        "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
        "text": "At 148 absence spells, the minimum detectable correlation (rho) at 80% power is approximately 0.23; true effect sizes smaller than this are unlikely to be detected."
      },
      {
        "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
        "text": "Reorganization metrics such as D_real and the structural influence L are estimated from play-type classifications that contain noise, and for short spells the within-absence play-type mix may be too sparse to measure reliably."
      },
      {
        "code": "CONFOUNDING",
        "text": "PPP change is influenced by the missing player's own scoring ability, opponent defensive strength during the absence, home/away schedule, and possible regression to the mean after an injury; failing to fully control these may produce spurious predictions."
      },
      {
        "code": "MULTIPLE_TESTING",
        "text": "Multiple candidate formulations of 'reorganization relative to L' (ratio, interaction, residual) may inflate Type I error unless a single primary metric is pre-registered."
      }
    ]
  },
  "prior_work": {
    "already_answered": "unknown",
    "cited": [],
    "summary": "No prior project studies addressing this question were available."
  },
  "risks": [
    {
      "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
      "text": "148 spells yield 80% power only for rho >= ~0.23; a more typical effect (e.g., rho = 0.1–0.15) would be missed."
    },
    {
      "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
      "text": "D_real and L both rely on inferring play types from play-by-play; classification errors and small game samples reduce reliability of the predictor and outcome."
    },
    {
      "code": "CONFOUNDING",
      "text": "Missing player quality, opponent strength, rest, and injury-related regression to the mean all affect PPP change independently of reorganization."
    },
    {
      "code": "MULTIPLE_TESTING",
      "text": "Without pre-registration, exploring multiple definitions of reorganization and PPP windows risks a large multiplicity correction or false positives."
    },
    {
      "code": "RTM_SELECTION",
      "text": "Absences often follow periods of unusually high or low team performance; PPP change may partly reflect regression to the mean rather than reorganization."
    },
    {
      "code": "EXPOSURE_SPARSITY",
      "text": "Long absence spells are rare; the sample may be dominated by a few teams or star players, limiting generalizability."
    }
  ],
  "predicted_outcome": {
    "supported": 0.15,
    "rejected": 0.20,
    "untestable": 0.25,
    "artifact": 0.15,
    "uncertain": 0.25
  },
  "decision": "proceed_with_changes",
  "design": {
    "estimand": "The predictive partial correlation (or standardized regression coefficient) between offensive reorganization (D_real, or D_real/L) during a player's absence and the change in team points per possession (adjusted for opponent and context), controlling for pre-absence PPP and the missing player's structural load L.",
    "unit": "A single player absence spell of at least 5 consecutive regular-season games missed (allowing rest days and minor injuries to be excluded). Only spells where the player played in >= 10 pre-absence games in the same season/team are included.",
    "sample": "All such spells from the 2022-23 through 2025-26 NBA regular seasons (extended to playoffs only as a secondary sensitivity check). Exact n expected ≈ 148 spells but will be confirmed after final exclusion rules.",
    "power": "With n ≈ 148 and one primary predictor (after controlling for 3–5 covariates), power ≥ 80% to detect a partial correlation ≥ 0.24 at alpha = 0.05 (two-sided). For the pre-specified primary test, the minimum detectable effect size is reported as rho_partial = 0.24. Sensitivity analyses with shuffled absence windows are used to estimate the empirical noise floor.",
    "controls": "Linear regression with covariates: pre-absence team offensive rating (full season excluding the absence player), average opponent defensive rating during the absence, share of absence games at home, and the missing player's offensive BPM or usage (to separate individual quality from structural load). Structural load L will be included as a main effect if the predictor is D_real alone; if using D_real/L, L still enters as a denominator sensitivity term.",
    "falsification": "1) Random-pseudos-spell test: randomly shift each absence window to a period when the player was active, recompute D_real and PPP change, and verify the predictive signal disappears. 2) Split-sample prediction: train on early seasons and test on later seasons to check overfitting. 3) Test whether reorganization predicts PPP change that occurs *before* the absence (should be null).",
    "multiplicity": "The primary predictor is (D_real / L) calculated from the teammate play-type mix displacement during the absence spell. All other formulations (residuals, interaction terms, alternative denominators) are demoted to exploratory and reported with full Bonferroni adjustment. The outcome is the change in points per 100 possessions, winsorized at the 1st and 99th percentiles.",
    "decision_rule": "The primary test is the two-sided t-test on the partial regression coefficient for D_real/L in the fully controlled model. The hypothesis is supported if the coefficient is statistically significantly different from zero (p < 0.05 after the single primary test) and the 95% CI excludes zero. The null is considered rejected (adequately powered) if the 95% CI lies entirely within a pre-specified equivalence bound of ±0.1 standardized points per 100 possessions per unit of D_real/L. Otherwise the result is uncertain.",
    "data_version": "NBA stats API per-game data as of 2026-11-01, plus derived play-type possession records; analysis will be version-locked in a reproducibility archive."
  }
}}

